  <div class="view" id="view-tips">
  <div class="pagehead"><h1>Tips</h1><p>What the signals mean, how to make your own writing sound like you, and how to use detectors without being misled.</p></div>
  <section id="tips">
    <div class="card use" style="margin-bottom:16px;border-top:4px solid var(--accent)">
      <h3 style="font-size:1.3rem;margin-bottom:6px">Getting the most out of Trace</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><b>Paste a full passage.</b> Aim for 100+ words. Short snippets give unreliable scores, and Trace will tell you when a text is too short.</li>
        <li><b>Keep your paragraph breaks.</b> Trace uses paragraph and sentence structure as signals, so pasting plain text without line breaks hides useful information.</li>
        <li><b>Click Analyze text.</b> You'll get an overall percentage, a signal breakdown, and highlighted sentences.</li>
        <li><b>Look beyond the number.</b> The breakdown shows which signals pushed the score and why. The highlighted sentences show exactly where the AI-like patterns are.</li>
        <li><b>Rate the result.</b> Use the buttons under each result and your scorecard on the Feedback page will show how well Trace matches your own judgment.</li>
        <li><b>Not sure how it behaves?</b> Use the two sample buttons on the analyzer to see what a clear AI-style and human-style text look like.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="zones">
        <div><b style="color:var(--human)">0–35% · Human</b>Mostly varied, personal, specific writing.</div>
        <div><b style="color:var(--mid)">35–65% · Uncertain</b>Mixed, edited, formal or short text often lands here.</div>
        <div><b style="color:var(--ai)">65–100% · AI</b>Even rhythm, stock phrases and formulaic structure.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="grid3">
      <div class="card tip">
        <div class="tag">Spotting</div>
        <h3>Telltale signs of AI text</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Even rhythm.</b> Sentences of similar length, with little variation (low "burstiness").</li>
          <li><b>Stock phrases.</b> "Delve into", "tapestry", "it's important to note", "in today's fast-paced world".</li>
          <li><b>Tidy structure.</b> Every paragraph the same size, a neat intro, three points, a "In conclusion".</li>
          <li><b>Frequent transitions.</b> Moreover, Furthermore, Additionally at sentence starts.</li>
          <li><b>"Not just X, it's Y"</b> constructions and heavy em dashes.</li>
          <li><b>No lived detail.</b> Few specific names, dates, anecdotes, or opinions.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card tip">
        <div class="tag">Writing</div>
        <h3>Make your own writing sound like you</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Mix short punchy sentences with long winding ones.</li>
          <li>Add specifics only you know: names, places, numbers, a small story.</li>
          <li>Take a position. Models hedge; people commit.</li>
          <li>Use contractions and your natural vocabulary.</li>
          <li>Cut filler openers and closers. Skip the summary paragraph.</li>
          <li>Read it aloud. If you'd never say it, rewrite it.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card tip">
        <div class="tag">Caution</div>
        <h3>Using detectors responsibly</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>No detector is certain.</b> Treat results as a hint, never proof.</li>
          <li><b>False positives happen,</b> especially for non-native English writers and formal or technical prose.</li>
          <li>Short texts (under ~100 words) are unreliable.</li>
          <li>Lightly edited AI text, or AI-assisted human text, is genuinely hard to classify.</li>
          <li>Never make punitive decisions on a score alone. Ask for drafts, version history, or a conversation.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card" style="margin-top:16px">
      <h3 style="font-size:1.25rem;margin-bottom:8px">How Trace works</h3>
      <details open><summary>What are the 8 signals?</summary><p>Burstiness (sentence-length variation), AI-favoured vocabulary, transition-word openers, informal markers (contractions, slang, first person), em dash and semicolon density, paragraph and sentence-opener uniformity, formulaic constructions, and chat-style formatting. Each is scored 0–100% for "AI-likeness" and combined with weights, then adjusted for text length.</p></details>
      <details><summary>How is the final percentage calculated?</summary><p>Each signal gets its own 0–100% score, and the signals are combined with different weights (AI-favoured wording and rhythm count most). The result is then adjusted for text length, so short texts are pulled toward 50% instead of giving a falsely confident answer. When the optional second-opinion check is switched on, its view is blended in and shown as a separate number so you can compare the two.</p></details>
      <details><summary>How accurate is it?</summary><p>Honestly: good on clear-cut cases (raw chatbot output vs. casual human writing), shaky on edited, formal, short, or mixed text. No tool, including commercial ones, can reach 100%. Confidence badges tell you when to trust a result less.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Is my text stored?</summary><p>The pattern analysis runs entirely in your browser and nothing is uploaded. If you leave the optional second-opinion toggle on, the text is also sent for one extra check on that request; untick it to keep everything on your device. Your ratings and feedback are saved only in your own browser.</p></details><details><summary>Why do I get different results for similar texts?</summary><p>Trace measures style, not authorship. Small changes such as adding contractions, varying sentence length or removing stock phrases can move the score, which is why the breakdown matters more than the single number.</p></details>
    </div>
